186045417] [17592186152079 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152079 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152079 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1045 "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186152923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152377 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fb0fa30b-881f-56fa-a0e3-9ebae5ac20bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152453 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x=1$\n- $x<0$\n- $|x|<5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aa2864d-150b-53f6-8540-2485ff225138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152453 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x=1)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-3,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x\\ne 1.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x<0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=1,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x≮0.$\n\n- Condition III is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ satisfies $|x|<5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152455 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152455 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152455 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}+2n-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n=1$\n- $n<0$\n- $|n|<5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1189e0d-f1d2-59f2-9035-c87ca64ef4f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152455 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =0 \\\\ (n+3)(n-1) & =0 \\\\ n & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=1)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=1,$ then ${n}^{2}+2n-3=0.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n<0)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =(-1)^{2}+2(-1)-3 \\\\  & =1-2-3 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<5)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+2n-3=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then $|n|<5,$ but ${n}^{2}+2n-3\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152923 #edb/ref 1061 144950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e11d65a7-2521-5479-b285-2b79997d9e03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152924 #edb/ref 1061 144951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c38c14fb-17e9-5f85-991b-ac6fcc11938e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152925 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152925 #edb/ref 1061 144954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b0274e4-9379-5bd8-af56-75d05a2a3640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152926 #edb/ref 1061 144958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48e01a17-a585-5104-b9a4-bafd11c635c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152927 #edb/ref 1061 144962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8943f5ca-c1e2-5d7c-afb1-72c562a7f753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152928 #edb/ref 1061 144963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c964453f-f719-562b-a613-a11fb6b95bfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153432 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the truth table for $S \\Rightarrow N$ below. Note that the implication is true $({\\color{blue}\\textrm{T}})$ in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows. \n\n| $S$ | $N$ | $S \\Rightarrow N$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\textrm T$ | $\\color{red}\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n\nOf the three rows where the implication is true, $S$ is true $({\\color{red}\\textrm{T}})$ only in the $1$st row, and $N$ is also true $({\\color{red}\\textrm{T}})$ in this row. \n\nSo, if the implication is true, $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is called a **sufficient condition** for $N.$\n\nOn the other hand, if $S$ is true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is also true. Therefore, $N$ is called a **necessary condition** for $S.$ \nHowever, $N$ being true *does not guarantee* that $S$ is true, as we can see from the $3$rd row in the truth table.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textbf{S}\\textrm{ufficient} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textbf{N}\\textrm{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nLet's look at some examples:\n\n- Consider the following true implication:\n\n$$\na > 0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a^2 > 0\n$$\n\nWe can label each part of the statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \n\\: \\Rightarrow \\: \n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a^2 > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- $a > 0$ is a *sufficient* condition for $a^2 > 0.$  In other words, $a > 0$ *guarantees* that $a^2 > 0.$\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is a *necessary* condition for $a > 0.$ In other words, if $a > 0,$ it is necessary that $a^2 > 0.$ However, $a^2 > 0$ *does not guarantee* that $a > 0.$\n\n- Suppose your teacher promises that *if* you score at least $90$ on the exam, *then* you will receive an $A.$ We can express this promise as the following implication:\n![](images/1b/1b2bcf888775f0be1dfc6d7b85c546c2f4c535bc0f04a7dadaceb06a0ab8110a.svg)\nLet's assume the teacher is telling the truth, so the implication is true. We can label each part of our implication as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b0dccacbb0c14d3b7c9a7a69f1cddda6b3a464bb2ae86b95989798d931d71d3.svg)\nTherefore:\n\n- Scoring at least $90$ on the exam is a *sufficient* condition for getting an $A.$ In other words, scoring at least $90$ *guarantees* that you'll get an $A.$\n\n- Getting an $A$ is a *necessary* condition for scoring at least $90.$ In other words, if you score at least $90,$ it necessarily follows that you'll receive $A.$ \n\nHowever, getting an $A$ *does not guarantee* that you scored at least $90$ on the exam. For example, the teacher may have decided to award an $A$ despite a score smaller than 90 if the exam was particularly tough that year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153432 #edb/ref 1065 8001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3e91a89c-375e-5c19-8d50-7a5d47800579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153444 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that an equivalence $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true. Then, we have the following terminology:\n\n- $P$ is a **necessary and sufficient condition** for $Q$\n\n- $Q$ is a **necessary and sufficient condition** for $P$\n\nFor example, consider the following two predicates:\n\n- $35 \\mid n$\n\n- $5 \\mid n$ and $7 \\mid n$\n\nThese are both necessary and sufficient conditions for each other. \n\nIndeed, recall that a number is divisible by $35$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $5$ and $7.$ In other words, we have\n\n![](images/f8/f850149a190068a3acc1225f6d56a73faef368e45ef69c1878eb933f94bc2f2b.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/7e/7eddbd9e552b236b3beca479b9f1e1d702709797dd087fe5d365af5ddac19ef2.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/e6/e6d8d5fa95eee18645f6c7fed335d31cc4816900b91cb8697662b5cdf106b0ba.svg)\n\nIn words:\n\n- $\\big( 35 \\mid n \\big)$ *is necessary and sufficient for* ![](images/de/de6cda8624f7ab07fab89d309b47cd0b8908aa5c04a9f5cb142add5f2403ab6a.svg)\n\n- ![](images/0e/0e2189b2579fb028fb41d1eeb58494cdb591315f6b0ac0bae72ed848479b879a.svg) *is necessary and sufficient for* $\\big( 35 \\mid n \\big).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153444 #edb/ref 1065 8321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153444 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "87478f3c-00be-545c-b9ce-1618a66bf0b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153444 #edb/ref 1067 "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420587 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420587 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420587 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0141819d-fce9-5dfe-8363-7f37fca55fde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420588 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/re